{% extends "layouts/docs" %}

{% block content %}
<p>Kiln escapes values from your code by default. When a function or filter returns HTML you trust, mark it as safe. When you pass class instances to templates, choose which members templates can use.</p>

<h2>Returning safe HTML</h2>

<p>Wrap a string in <code>safe()</code> to output it without escaping:</p>

{% apply highlight("ts") %}
{% verbatim %}
import { createKiln, safe } from "@lucidcms/kiln";

const kiln = createKiln({
	loader,
	filters: {
		markdown: (text: string) => safe(renderMarkdown(text)),
	},
});
{% endverbatim %}
{% endapply %}

{% apply highlight("kiln") %}
{% verbatim %}
<article>{{ post.body|markdown }}</article>
{% endverbatim %}
{% endapply %}

<p>Only mark output safe when your code produced it or cleaned it. The second argument sets the strategy it is safe for, such as <code>safe(value, "js")</code>. It defaults to <code>"html"</code>.</p>

<p><code>isSafe(value)</code> checks whether a value is safe markup. Safe values are instances of the <code>Markup</code> class, and convert to strings as normal.</p>

<h2>Class instances</h2>

<p>Templates can read plain objects and arrays freely. A class instance exposes nothing until its class opts in, so templates can't reach methods or internals you didn't intend. List the members templates may use with the <code>expose</code> symbol:</p>

{% apply highlight("ts") %}
{% verbatim %}
import { expose } from "@lucidcms/kiln";

class Post {
	static [expose] = ["title", "url", "excerpt"] as const;

	constructor(private data: PostData) {}

	get title() {
		return this.data.title;
	}

	get url() {
		return `/blog/${this.data.slug}/`;
	}

	excerpt(length: number) {
		return this.data.body.slice(0, length);
	}
}
{% endverbatim %}
{% endapply %}

{% apply highlight("kiln") %}
{% verbatim %}
<a href="{{ post.url }}">{{ post.title }}</a>
<p>{{ post.excerpt(120) }}</p>
{% endverbatim %}
{% endapply %}

<ul>
	<li>Listed getters and fields can be read. Listed methods can be called.</li>
	<li><code>expose</code> is read from the class and its parent classes. In a base class meant to be extended, type the list as <code>readonly string[]</code> rather than <code>as const</code>.</li>
	<li>List <code>toString</code> to let templates print the object.</li>
	<li>Dates, sets and maps work without opting in.</li>
</ul>

<h2>Dynamic fields</h2>

<p>For fields you don't know in advance, such as custom fields from a CMS, add a <code>lookup</code> method. It answers any name not in <code>expose</code>. Return <code>undefined</code> for a missing field.</p>

{% apply highlight("ts") %}
{% verbatim %}
import { expose, lookup } from "@lucidcms/kiln";

class Post {
	static [expose] = ["title"] as const;

	[lookup](name: string): unknown {
		return this.fields.get(name);
	}
}
{% endverbatim %}
{% endapply %}

<p>Names such as <code>constructor</code>, <code>prototype</code> and <code>__proto__</code> stay blocked whatever <code>expose</code> and <code>lookup</code> say.</p>
{% endblock %}
